# Links that resolve fine for a human browser but trip the automated checker
# (anti-bot blocks, HTTP/2 quirks, aggressive rate-limiting). One regex per line.
# If you add one here, verify by hand that it really works, and note why.

# RFC 2606 example domain — used in illustrative snippets, 404 by design.
example\.com

# BusinessWire press releases: server negotiates HTTP/2 badly and lychee errors
# out on the connection. The URL is reachable in a normal browser.
businesswire\.com

# GlobeNewswire press releases: same failure mode as BusinessWire — the server
# errors out on HTTP/2 for automated clients and times out for datacenter IPs.
# Verified by hand 2026-09-03: the cited URL returns HTTP 200 with the right
# article title in a real browser. Anti-bot artifact, not a dead link.
globenewswire\.com

# aitoolkitpro.blog: times out for GitHub Actions runners, and once errored on a
# cached failure from the same run. Verified by hand 2026-09-04 from a normal
# client — three consecutive requests, HTTP 200 in ~1.7s each, correct article.
# Alive and fast for a reader; unreachable from CI datacenter IPs. Two runs in a
# row failed the whole check on this one host while the other 989 links passed.
aitoolkitpro\.blog

# almcorp.com: same signature as aitoolkitpro.blog below — two consecutive runs
# failed on this one host (timeout, then timeout + cached error) while the other
# 987 links passed. Verified by hand 2026-09-04 from a normal client: three
# consecutive requests, HTTP 200 in ~1.7s each, correct article. Alive for a
# reader, unreachable from CI datacenter IPs.
# NOTE: this is the second "industry source" blog in one day to behave this way.
# The claim it supports — that Claude returns structured citations with clickable
# source URLs — is observable product behaviour, and the plan is to replace it
# with a dated first-hand observation. Ignoring the link keeps CI honest in the
# meantime; it does not make the citation stronger.
almcorp\.com
